// Original extensions: explanation, discrimination and transfer to unfamiliar scenarios.
export const depth={
'capture-at-source':{
 sections:[['Design the capture transaction','Treat capture as a controlled transaction rather than a button press. Specify the item boundary, business identifier, source channel, event date, destination, owner and acknowledgement. An email may be one message plus attachments and thread context; a scanned case may contain several document types. Decide which relationships must remain intact. Where a channel can send the same item twice, use a stable identifier and a reconciliation rule. A retry should not create a second payable invoice. Conversely, rejecting every repeated filename can discard genuinely different items. The control must distinguish duplicate delivery from legitimate new evidence.'],['Measure the entire chain','A scanner producing 200 pages per minute can feed a validation queue that handles only 40. The queue becomes the constraint. Measure arrival volume, successful capture, exceptions, elapsed time and rework at each handoff. Quality assurance designs a process capable of reliable output; quality control checks particular outputs. Formal acceptance then confirms the resulting information meets the intended business purpose. These are related but distinct activities. A visual sample can miss a systematic metadata-mapping error, so combine image inspection, automated validation and business reconciliation. Select sampling and escalation according to consequence, not a convenient uniform percentage.']],
 compare:[['Day-forward','New information enters a controlled process immediately.','Does not by itself reduce the historical backlog.'],['Backfile','A selected historical collection is converted in a planned project.','Preparation, indexing and exception costs can exceed scan costs.'],['On demand','Conversion follows actual retrieval requests.','Urgent requests need capacity and turnaround rules.'],['Hybrid','Different collections receive different approaches.','Selection criteria and ownership must remain explicit.']],
 case:{situation:'A distributor receives 2,000 delivery packets daily through email, a portal and paper. The same packet may arrive twice. Customer claims require a signed proof of delivery, not every duplicate cover page. The team proposes one large scanner and plans to review image quality after the backlog is cleared.',question:'What should the design establish before equipment is selected?',reasoning:'Identify the business packet and required evidence, choose the earliest reliable capture points for each channel, establish a stable delivery identifier and define duplicate handling. Validate signatures or required fields according to the task, reconcile source totals to accepted packets, and assign exceptions. Estimate preparation and validation capacity as well as scanning speed.',alternatives:'Scanning first scales the unresolved identification problem. Deleting every apparent duplicate by filename risks losing distinct evidence. Keeping all deliveries forever avoids neither missing packets nor excessive retention.'},
 checks:[['Why can batch file counts match while evidence is missing?','A file can contain omitted pages, mixed cases or repeated images. Counts must be combined with item/page reconciliation, identity checks and risk-appropriate content validation.'],['A central capture team is accurate but delays intake decisions. Is centralization necessarily best?','No. Compare local capture at the decision point with shared quality rules and central monitoring. The answer depends on timeliness, skill, control and exception requirements.']]
},
'document-control':{
 sections:[['Separate state from history','A document has content, versions, workflow state and permissions. These dimensions change independently. Version 6 might be the latest draft while version 4 remains the approved publication. Major/minor numbering is a convention, not proof of authorization. Define who can change status, whether approval applies to a specific immutable version and what happens when an approved document is edited. A check-in comment explains a change, while an audit event records an action; neither automatically establishes substantive approval. Users need a clear authoritative view without losing necessary history.'],['Evaluate governance in realistic use','Use a requirements-to-evidence matrix during selection. For each important requirement, specify the scenario, configuration, expected outcome and acceptance evidence. Test revoked guest access, conflicting edits, a restored version, a missing approval, an export and a hold. Include who operates each control after the vendor leaves. Broader platforms can reduce fragmentation, but customization and integrations create their own maintenance burden. A specialized application may be appropriate when it preserves required evidence and interoperates with lifecycle controls. Evaluate the implemented capability rather than treating ECM, DMS or file sharing as guarantees of a particular control set.']],
 compare:[['Version control','Explains and retrieves changes over time.','Latest is not necessarily approved.'],['Check-out','Coordinates exclusive editing when required.','Can obstruct coauthoring if applied indiscriminately.'],['Approval','Records authorized acceptance of a defined version.','A version number does not replace it.'],['Audit trail','Records relevant actions and actors.','Needs integrity, access and retention controls itself.']],
 case:{situation:'A manufacturer publishes controlled procedures to technicians. Authors need simultaneous editing. Technicians must see the current approved version even while a replacement is being drafted. The proposed rule gives everyone edit rights and treats the newest timestamp as authoritative.',question:'How should the states and permissions be designed?',reasoning:'Separate drafting from publication. Permit appropriate coauthoring in the draft workspace, require approval of a defined version, and publish a clearly identified approved rendition to the technician audience. Retain required history and evidence of approval. Test that a new draft cannot silently replace the operative procedure and that superseded versions are handled under approved rules.',alternatives:'Mandatory exclusive check-out everywhere can impair authors without solving publication authority. Giving all users edit rights weakens integrity. A backup can recover a file but does not determine which version was authorized.'},
 checks:[['A user restores an old version. Should its previous approval automatically make it current?','Not automatically. Restoration and current authorization are separate events. Apply the organization’s reapproval and publication rules to the restored content.'],['What evidence distinguishes a real requirement demonstration from a product promise?','A documented test of the required behavior, including configuration, permissions, exceptions and expected results, accepted by accountable stakeholders.']]
},
'collaboration':{
 sections:[['Preserve decisions across collaboration channels','Separate working conversation from decisions that commit the organization. A decision record should identify what was decided, who had authority, when it occurred, the relevant version and supporting context. Do not assume a complete meeting recording is the only or best evidence. Chat, email, shared documents and meetings can all contribute. Define how decisions move into the authoritative process without making users duplicate every conversation. For asynchronous work, clear status, ownership and response expectations prevent silence from being mistaken for approval. For synchronous work, capture the outcome for absent participants and future explanation.'],['Control the external boundary','Sharing creates a boundary between organizational control models. Establish who owns the workspace, how guests are authenticated, what access expires, whether downloads are permitted and what evidence must return at closure. Revoking a link does not retrieve a copy already downloaded. A supplier may apply a different retention rule or delete an account when a contract ends. Address export formats, metadata, attachments, decision histories and responsibility before the project reaches closure. Controls must be proportionate: excessively difficult collaboration encourages personal email and unsanctioned storage, creating less visible risk.']],
 compare:[['Synchronous','Useful for immediate clarification and complex disagreement.','Requires availability and a durable decision record.'],['Asynchronous','Useful across time zones and for considered review.','Needs deadlines, version references and explicit responses.'],['Attachment','Creates an independently circulating copy.','Recall and version consistency are difficult.'],['Controlled shared link','References an access-controlled source item.','Downloaded copies and inherited permissions still matter.']],
 case:{situation:'A project spans three countries and a partner-owned workspace. Different teams review different downloaded versions. A manager says the decision was approved on a video call, but the absent legal reviewer cannot identify the text that was approved. Partner access ends Friday.',question:'What controls address the immediate and continuing problem?',reasoning:'Resolve the authoritative version and approval evidence with the accountable decision owner. Capture the relevant outcome and supporting materials before access ends. Establish explicit version-based review and a closure export/verification process. For future work, combine asynchronous review with targeted meetings, making the final decision and its authority visible to all required participants.',alternatives:'Recording every future meeting does not repair the missing version association. Keeping the partner account indefinitely may be neither permitted nor reliable. A blanket ban on collaboration tools can push work into harder-to-govern channels.'},
 checks:[['Why can a project deliverable be insufficient evidence?','It may omit why a choice was made, who authorized it, conditions attached to approval or relevant alternatives and objections. Retain context according to business and recordkeeping needs.'],['What should a guest-access review examine beyond the named guest list?','Inherited groups, shared links, subcontractor access, exported copies, access duration and the information each permission actually exposes.']]
},
'preservation':{
 sections:[['Build a chain of preservation evidence','Distinguish the original object, a preservation copy and an access rendition. A smaller access copy may be convenient without replacing the version required as evidence. Record identifiers, source, format, transformations, dates, responsible parties and validation results. Fixity means the bits match an expected reference. Authenticity concerns whether an object is what it purports to be, supported by provenance and controls. Usability concerns whether people can interpret it for the intended purpose. A collection can pass fixity checks yet fail because fonts, links, encryption keys or application dependencies are missing.'],['Choose and validate transformations','Migration can alter layout, formulas, metadata, links or behavior. Decide which significant properties must survive and test them explicitly. A spreadsheet preserved as an image may show values but lose formulas essential to explaining a calculation. Emulation can preserve interactions while increasing dependency on an older environment and its software rights. Open, documented formats can reduce dependence but still need testing, storage management and monitoring. Geographic redundancy can improve resilience, yet synchronized corruption can affect multiple copies. Maintain independently recoverable copies and test restoration and rendering, not only replication status.']],
 compare:[['Fixity','Checks bits against a reference.','Cannot prove truth or meaningful rendering.'],['Provenance','Documents origin and transformations.','Requires trustworthy capture and ongoing management.'],['Migration','Moves content into a new supported form.','Needs validation of significant properties.'],['Emulation','Recreates an environment to retain behavior.','Requires sustainable dependencies and testing.']],
 case:{situation:'An engineering archive contains interactive models, linked specifications and a proprietary viewer. Staff propose printing every model to PDF, retaining the PDFs on redundant storage and deleting the source models when the viewer license expires.',question:'What must be decided before treating that plan as preservation?',reasoning:'Identify which properties are necessary: fixed appearance may be insufficient if users must inspect geometry, layers or relationships. Inventory dependencies and rights, evaluate supported exports or emulation, and validate representative complex objects. Preserve provenance and required source material while deciding. Redundancy protects stored bits but cannot restore behavior that was discarded during conversion.',alternatives:'Keeping every old application running without a support plan also leaves risk. Selecting an archival label alone does not establish that the chosen format represents the required properties. Deleting source objects before validation removes options.'},
 checks:[['Can an accessible rendition differ from the preserved original?','Yes, if the relationship and provenance are clear and the original or preservation representation is retained as required. Different purposes can justify different representations.'],['Why test a complete collection rather than opening one sample file?','Dependencies, embedded objects and unusual formats vary. Risk-based representative testing and exception analysis reveal failures a convenient sample can miss.']]
},
'ownership-and-selection':{
 sections:[['Define authority at the right level','An application can be authoritative for transaction status while another repository is authoritative for signed agreements. Authority must be defined by information type and purpose, not merely declared for an entire platform. Identify who resolves conflicts and what consumers may rely on. A master dataset needs quality and update responsibilities; a system of record needs dependable evidence and retrieval. Map downstream copies so correction, restriction and disposition decisions can propagate appropriately. Where integration is delayed, define how users recognize temporary inconsistencies and which source wins for the decision in question.'],['Appraise before moving','Selection should distinguish useful redundancy from accidental duplication. An identical file may be a convenience copy, but a copy with unique annotations may carry separate evidence. Assess business value, obligations, holds, historical significance, cost and retrieval need. Profile unknown material rather than assuming age equals uselessness. Record why categories are moved, retained in the legacy system, archived or disposed of. If read-only legacy access is selected, fund and own it; otherwise selective migration can create an unsupported shadow archive. Validate exclusions as well as transferred content because an omitted category may be discovered only during an audit.']],
 compare:[['Authoritative source','Designated evidence or reference for a defined purpose.','Need not own every representation of the information.'],['Substantive draft','May explain negotiation, decisions or authorization.','Draft status alone does not permit deletion.'],['Convenience copy','Used without adding distinct evidential value.','Check annotations, obligations and holds first.'],['Read-only legacy access','Avoids immediate conversion of some material.','Still needs support, security and retrieval ownership.']],
 case:{situation:'A merger brings two customer repositories and a file share. Sales wants the newest address, legal needs the address used on each signed contract, and marketing wants duplicate contacts removed. A proposal overwrites historical addresses with the newest value and deletes every older spreadsheet.',question:'How should authority and selection be separated?',reasoning:'Define current operational address authority separately from historical transaction evidence. Preserve the address and context associated with executed agreements while reconciling the current customer master. Appraise spreadsheets for unique content and obligations before disposal. Choose migration mappings that preserve those distinct purposes rather than forcing one current value to stand in for all history.',alternatives:'Keeping all copies without reconciliation preserves ambiguity. Deduplicating only by customer name can merge different people or organizations. Overwriting historical context can undermine evidence even when the new address is correct today.'},
 checks:[['When is selective migration preferable to moving everything?','When scope is justified through value, obligations, risk and verified retrieval arrangements, and exclusions are documented and approved. It is not a shortcut for ignoring unknown content.'],['Who should decide which conflicting customer field is authoritative?','The accountable business owner with relevant stakeholders and control specialists; technical teams implement the agreed authority and reconciliation rules.']]
},
};
Object.assign(depth,{
'metadata':{
 sections:[['Model meaning before fields','For each field, define the business meaning, datatype, allowed values, cardinality, source, responsible owner and update rule. Cardinality specifies whether one or several values are allowed. A unique identifier should remain stable when labels or locations change. Distinguish a date of creation, upload, business completion and retention trigger. They may coincide in a simple case and diverge later. Describe whether a field is historical or current: department at creation and current steward answer different questions. Without these definitions, apparently successful mapping can change meaning while preserving attractive column names.'],['Control quality and propagation','Completeness asks whether required values are present; validity asks whether they meet defined rules; accuracy asks whether they reflect reality; consistency asks whether related representations agree. A valid-looking date can still be inaccurate. Measure defects by field and business consequence. Inherited values need provenance and re-evaluation when a parent changes. Map controlled vocabularies explicitly, including deprecated terms, unknown values and many-to-one relationships. Decide whether a change propagates to existing items or only future ones. Masking or restricting display can protect a value, but does not repair its correctness or remove the need to govern the underlying data.']],
 compare:[['Mandatory field','Blocks incomplete submission.','Users may invent values if the information is unavailable.'],['Controlled vocabulary','Constrains labels to agreed choices.','The wrong permitted choice can still be selected.'],['Inheritance','Reduces repeated entry from a trusted context.','Can spread incorrect or stale defaults.'],['Business validation','Checks against events or authoritative references.','Requires defined sources and exception ownership.']],
 case:{situation:'A migration maps a multi-valued jurisdiction field into a single text field and imports folder creation date as contract end date. Every target record passes the import schema. Retention and access rules then behave inconsistently.',question:'Why did technically valid data fail operationally?',reasoning:'The mappings changed business meaning and cardinality. Preserve multiple applicable jurisdictions through an appropriate target model, and obtain the genuine lifecycle event rather than substituting a convenient technical timestamp. Test downstream policy behavior with representative records. Route missing events to exceptions instead of inventing apparently valid values.',alternatives:'Making the target fields mandatory would not correct their meaning. Adding more free text can obscure controlled relationships. Repeating the import with the same mappings reproduces the same defect.'},
 checks:[['A field is 100% populated and every entry matches its format. Is it accurate?','Not necessarily. Completeness and validity do not establish agreement with the real business facts. Compare against authoritative evidence and sample according to risk.'],['Why preserve an identifier when a taxonomy label changes?','The stable identifier maintains relationships, audit history and cross-system links while the human-readable label can evolve.']]
},
'taxonomy':{
 sections:[['Separate concepts, labels and relationships','A concept is the underlying meaning; a label is a term used to name it. Synonyms can name one concept, while the same word can mean different concepts in different contexts. Hierarchical broader/narrower relationships support navigation; associative relationships connect relevant concepts without claiming a parent-child relationship. Facets describe independent dimensions, such as business function, location and document type, allowing several retrieval paths without forcing all information into one folder tree. Define terms precisely enough that users can classify representative material consistently.'],['Operate vocabulary change','Collect terms from users, search logs, existing classifications and control requirements. Test common and difficult cases with several classifiers. Disagreement can indicate an ambiguous definition, overlapping concepts or inadequate training. Assign a vocabulary owner and a documented process for new, renamed, merged and retired concepts. Preserve mappings so older items remain findable. Avoid automatically merging two concepts because their labels resemble each other. Where classification drives retention or security, assess the policy impact before a vocabulary update changes existing content.']],
 compare:[['Hierarchy','Organizes broader and narrower concepts.','A single branch may not represent every useful dimension.'],['Thesaurus','Adds controlled preferred, alternative and related terms.','A synonym is not simply any loosely related word.'],['Faceted classification','Combines independent descriptive dimensions.','Facets need consistent meanings and usable values.'],['Functional scheme','Groups enduring activities or purposes.','Still needs review when the business function changes.']],
 case:{situation:'A global firm uses the label charge for customer billing, legal allegations and battery status. Search suggestions treat every use as a synonym. Meanwhile, an organizational rebrand creates duplicate categories for the same HR function.',question:'How should the classification distinguish these situations?',reasoning:'Separate the different charge concepts using clear definitions and contextual labels. Treat the HR rebrand as a label change or controlled mapping where the underlying concept remains the same. Use stable identifiers, appropriate synonyms and facets rather than uncontrolled duplication. Pilot retrieval and policy application after the changes.',alternatives:'Merging all matching words damages meaning. Creating a fresh concept for every renamed department fragments history. Replacing classification entirely with keyword search does not solve policy mapping or ambiguous terminology.'},
 checks:[['How can two users navigate to the same item through different paths?','Apply independent facets and concept relationships so the item can be found by function, location or type without creating uncontrolled copies.'],['What must be checked before merging taxonomy categories?','Whether meanings, applicable policies, identifiers and existing assignments can be reconciled without losing distinctions or changing controls unintentionally.']]
},
'extraction':{
 sections:[['Distinguish detection from business acceptance','Recognition produces candidate text. Classification selects a document category. Field extraction identifies values and their locations. Normalization converts representations to an agreed form, such as a date or supplier identifier. Validation checks whether those values are acceptable in context. Preserve the source relationship so a reviewer can inspect the exact evidence behind an extracted field. A high confidence score means the model strongly favors its interpretation under its own method; it is not independent confirmation that the interpretation is correct or that the transaction is legitimate.'],['Set thresholds by consequences','Measure field-level performance, document-level completion and straight-through processing separately. A system may extract most fields correctly yet require intervention on nearly every document. False acceptance can be more costly than an exception when payment, identity or compliance depends on the field. Use representative layouts and degraded images, not only clean demonstration forms. Establish verification for high-impact values, reference checks, audit evidence and feedback for systematic defects. Repeated corrections should improve the process or source quality rather than remain an invisible permanent burden on reviewers.']],
 compare:[['OCR','Recognizes printed or handwritten character patterns.','Does not verify business meaning.'],['Barcode lookup','Connects a symbol to an identifier or reference.','A correctly read symbol may refer to the wrong packet.'],['Normalization','Standardizes equivalent representations.','Must not silently reinterpret an ambiguous date.'],['Validation','Tests values against rules and references.','Rules need an owner and a treatment for exceptions.']],
 case:{situation:'An engine reports 98% field accuracy on invoices. Each invoice has 20 fields and the organization rejects any invoice with a critical-field error. Reviewers still handle many documents, particularly new supplier layouts.',question:'Why can the headline accuracy coexist with high manual workload?',reasoning:'Field accuracy and complete-document success are different measures. Errors may be concentrated in critical fields or uncommon layouts, and several small risks combine across a document. Measure actual straight-through completion, exception categories and critical-field false acceptance on representative production data. Improve source/layout handling and targeted validation rather than assuming the 98% figure means 98% of documents require no review.',alternatives:'Lowering thresholds to force throughput can pass dangerous errors. More reviewers may absorb symptoms without fixing systematic layout problems. An average across easy and difficult fields can obscure the main business risk.'},
 checks:[['A normalized value looks correct but the original was ambiguous. What evidence should remain?','The source text or image relationship, transformation rule and review decision, so the interpretation can be checked and corrected.'],['What is straight-through processing?','Completion through the defined process without manual intervention. Its boundary and acceptance criteria must be specified; it is not the same as raw recognition accuracy.']]
},
'analytics':{
 sections:[['Work through the confusion matrix','A true positive is a correctly detected target; a false positive is an ordinary item incorrectly called a target; a false negative is a target that was missed. Precision equals true positives divided by all predicted positives. Recall equals true positives divided by all actual positives. Suppose a tool flags 100 files, 80 are sensitive, and the reference collection contains 120 sensitive files. Precision is 80/100, or 80%; recall is 80/120, about 66.7%. The remaining 40 sensitive files are false negatives. Changing a threshold affects both review workload and the risk of missed content.'],['Evaluate beyond one average','Use independent test data and avoid leakage, such as placing near-duplicate documents in both training and evaluation sets. Inspect performance across important classes, languages and operating conditions. Compare the model to a sensible baseline and the actual cost of mistakes. Monitor distribution changes, review overrides and investigate disagreement with human labels. A correlation useful for prediction does not demonstrate that changing one factor causes the predicted outcome. Keep the model’s intended decision separate from a more consequential decision it was never evaluated to make.']],
 compare:[['Precision','How many flagged items really belong to the target class?','High precision can coexist with many missed targets.'],['Recall','How many actual targets were found?','High recall can impose substantial review workload.'],['Accuracy','How many overall classifications are correct?','Can mislead when important classes are rare.'],['Drift monitoring','Checks whether data or behavior changes after deployment.','A successful launch test is not permanent assurance.']],
 case:{situation:'A sensitive-content detector has excellent precision but misses handwritten forms. Management wants it to authorize deletion of every unflagged document. The test set contained only machine-printed documents.',question:'Is that expansion justified?',reasoning:'No. Unflagged is not equivalent to nonsensitive, especially when recall on handwriting is unknown. Evaluate the relevant formats, use a representative reference set, consider the consequence of deletion and require appropriate review or safeguards. The detector’s demonstrated ability to identify some sensitive documents does not validate an irreversible disposal decision.',alternatives:'A higher overall accuracy score does not establish coverage of the missing class. Training on additional examples can help but still requires independent evaluation. Merely renaming the tool as a governance engine changes no evidence.'},
 checks:[['If 90 of 100 flagged files are relevant, but 300 relevant files exist, what are precision and recall?','Precision is 90%; recall is 90/300, or 30%. The result list is mostly relevant but misses most relevant files.'],['Why can random splitting still produce a misleading test?','Duplicates, related cases or time-dependent information can leak across the split. Evaluation must reflect the independence and future conditions of real use.']]
},
'search':{
 sections:[['Diagnose coverage, permissions and ranking separately','A source connector may fail to fetch an item, a parser may fail to extract its content, an index may omit useful metadata, or a ranking method may place a relevant item too low. A permission rule may correctly hide the item from the current user. Investigate these possibilities in order rather than treating every complaint as a ranking defect. Ask whether users need precise lookup, exploratory discovery or comprehensive collection; those goals require different measures and interfaces. Search quality depends on the corpus and the task, not merely on the engine’s feature list.'],['Design evaluation around real tasks','Build a small benchmark of realistic queries with known relevant material and appropriate user identities. Measure retrieval and task completion, including failed and ambiguous queries. Precision and recall require a defined reference set; clicks alone cannot prove relevance or completeness. Facets, synonyms, identifiers, descriptive metadata and understandable snippets can help users refine their search. Semantic retrieval can improve matching when wording differs, but it can also retrieve plausible yet irrelevant items. Apply current authorization to content and snippets, and ensure deletions, restrictions and corrections propagate to indexes and caches.']],
 compare:[['Exact lookup','Find a known item through a reliable identifier.','Ranking sophistication cannot repair a missing identifier.'],['Exploratory search','Learn the collection and refine a question.','Useful facets and context may matter as much as keywords.'],['Comprehensive collection','Seek all material within a defined scope.','An attractive first page does not demonstrate recall.'],['Security trimming','Limits results to authorized material.','Snippets and cached extracts also need protection.']],
 case:{situation:'A search engine returns ten highly relevant documents for an audit request, but the auditor expects 200 responsive items. The product team points to a high click-through rate as proof the search is successful.',question:'Which evidence is missing?',reasoning:'The task requires coverage, so establish the scope and reference set, then examine recall and collection completeness. Check all relevant sources, connectors, metadata, date boundaries and permissions. User clicks on a few good results do not establish that all responsive material was found. Keep the collection process and its limitations documented.',alternatives:'Increasing result-list length without repairing missing sources may add noise rather than coverage. Removing access restrictions can create disclosure risk. A different ranking algorithm cannot retrieve content never indexed.'},
 checks:[['A document opens by identifier but never appears in keyword results. What should be examined?','Parsed text, indexed fields, vocabulary, filters and ranking. Source access exists, but the relevant search representation may be absent or poorly matched.'],['Why test search under more than one user role?','Coverage and snippets must respect different permissions, and identity or group mapping errors may affect only particular roles.']]
},
'migration':{
 sections:[['Reconcile more than totals','Define a manifest identifying each source item, selected versions, relationships, expected metadata, permissions and required destination. A checksum is useful for unchanged binary transfer, but conversion may legitimately change bits; then compare the required content and significant properties instead. Account for selected, transferred, excluded, failed and retried items so totals reconcile without concealing exceptions. Duplicate target objects may reflect retries rather than legitimate versions. Validate linked objects, compound documents and attachments, because file-by-file success can still leave the collection unusable.'],['Design cutover as a business transition','A full freeze simplifies reconciliation but interrupts work. Delta migration moves changed items after an initial load and needs reliable change detection. Phased migration reduces the scope of each cutover but creates temporary split operations and dependency risks. Define rollback triggers, who decides, what happens to new changes and how users find authoritative information during the transition. Retire legacy access only after agreed validation and acceptance. External services require export rights, usable formats and continuing access arrangements before accounts close. A tool that can export files may still omit audit history or relationships.']],
 compare:[['Freeze and cut over','Creates a stable final source state.','Business interruption must be acceptable.'],['Delta synchronization','Transfers changes after the initial load.','Missed or reordered changes can create inconsistencies.'],['Phased migration','Moves controlled subsets over time.','Shared dependencies and dual operations need management.'],['Rollback','Returns operation to an agreed stable state.','New work after cutover must be reconciled, not discarded.']],
 case:{situation:'A team migrates 10,000 files overnight and users begin editing the target. The next morning, permission defects are discovered. The proposed rollback simply reopens the old system and deletes the target.',question:'What is wrong with that rollback?',reasoning:'It ignores changes created after cutover and can lose legitimate new work. A rollback plan must define the authoritative system, capture or reconcile target changes, restore appropriate access and communicate the transition. Investigate the scope of the permission defect and compare safe remediation with controlled rollback. Preserve evidence and user work throughout the decision.',alternatives:'Leaving broad permissions open until convenient is unsafe. Declaring success from file totals does not meet permission acceptance criteria. A rollback plan written only after failure leaves essential decisions unresolved.'},
 checks:[['When is a checksum mismatch expected?','When an approved transformation changes the binary representation. Validate the intended content and properties, and record the transformation rather than treating any changed hash as corruption.'],['What should be in an exception queue?','An identifiable item, failure reason, business impact, responsible owner, corrective action, status and verification evidence. Otherwise exceptions can disappear into summary totals.']]
},
'llm':{
 sections:[['Understand grounding and its limits','A retrieval-augmented answer has at least two failure points: selecting evidence and generating a supported response. Relevant evidence can be omitted, unauthorized evidence can be included, and a generated claim can go beyond the retrieved material. Evaluate whether each material claim is supported by the cited passage, not simply whether a citation link exists. State uncertainty or abstain when evidence is insufficient. Model knowledge and an organization’s current authoritative policy are different sources; a fluent general answer should not override a controlled policy document.'],['Constrain the operating process','Define the allowed task, information boundary, tools and required human approvals. Treat retrieved documents and user-provided content as untrusted data rather than instructions to expand permissions or disclose secrets. Record the model/configuration and enough evaluation context to investigate failures without logging unnecessary personal data. Test privacy leakage, misleading citations, stale evidence and malicious document instructions. Assess vendor input retention and location before supplying sensitive content. A human reviewer needs source access and decision criteria; a superficial approval click does not create meaningful oversight.']],
 compare:[['Summarization','Condenses supplied material.','Can omit qualifications or distort relationships.'],['Grounded question answering','Uses selected evidence to support a response.','Retrieval and claim support both require evaluation.'],['Content generation','Drafts possible wording or structure.','Plausibility is not factual verification.'],['Tool-using assistant','Can act through connected capabilities.','Actions need narrow authority and suitable approval gates.']],
 case:{situation:'An employee asks an assistant for a retention period. Search retrieves a retired policy, a vendor blog and a current approved schedule. The assistant quotes the old period and includes a valid link to the current schedule, although that schedule does not support the answer.',question:'What would make the workflow more trustworthy?',reasoning:'Prioritize authoritative current sources, preserve version/status metadata, require claim-level support and evaluate the response against the actual passage. Where categories or jurisdictions are unclear, ask for the missing context or route to a responsible specialist. An answer should explain its limits rather than filling gaps with a plausible period.',alternatives:'A larger model does not guarantee correct source selection. Adding more unfiltered documents can increase confusion. A disclaimer does not repair a materially unsupported answer.'},
 checks:[['Why is a source document capable of creating a security risk?','It can contain malicious instructions or sensitive material outside the requester’s authority. Enforce access before retrieval and prevent source text from changing the system’s authorized behavior.'],['What makes a useful evaluation case for an information assistant?','A realistic task with authoritative evidence, expected supported outcomes, known limits and failure conditions such as missing, stale or conflicting information.']]
},
});

Object.assign(depth,{
'process-discovery':{
 sections:[['Define the unit of work before measuring it','A process transforms a trigger and inputs into an outcome for a customer or other recipient. Name the start and finish precisely: receipt of an invoice to an approved payment instruction is different from receipt to settlement. Identify the process owner, participants, information, controls and systems. Observe actual work and sample cases rather than relying only on a procedure manual. Staff may compensate for missing information through spreadsheets or informal messages; those workarounds reveal requirements and risks that a polished diagram can hide.'],['Choose improvement candidates using evidence','High volume alone does not make a process a good automation candidate. Compare business value, repeatability, exception frequency, information quality, control exposure and readiness to change. Separate unnecessary activities from necessary controls before removing steps. A delay caused by unclear approval authority needs an ownership decision; software cannot decide who should be accountable. Investigate variation by case type and location. Averages can conceal a small group of complex cases that consumes most effort. Establish a baseline before changes so benefits can be evaluated.']],
 compare:[['Digitization','Changes an analog representation to digital.','Does not by itself improve the process.'],['Process improvement','Changes how work is performed.','Requires evidence of a better outcome.'],['Automation','Executes defined activities through technology.','Can accelerate a poorly designed process.'],['Transformation','Changes the operating model or service materially.','Requires organizational change, not only a new tool.']],
 case:{situation:'A purchasing team proposes automating all approvals because invoices take twelve days. Observation shows eight days waiting for missing purchase-order references and one day in approval. Several suppliers repeatedly submit incomplete documents.',question:'Where should the team intervene first?',reasoning:'Investigate why references are missing and improve capture, validation and supplier instructions. Segment exceptions and assign resolution ownership. Approval automation may still help, but cannot remove the dominant upstream delay. Measure elapsed time and exception rates before and after the intervention.',alternatives:'Buying a faster approval tool targets a small part of the delay. Eliminating all approvals ignores control requirements. Blaming staff without examining the input process misses the recurring cause.'},
 checks:[['What is the danger of measuring only completed cases?','Cases still waiting can disappear from the measurement. Track work in progress and aging alongside completion times to expose stalled work.'],['Why interview several roles?','Each role sees different handoffs and exceptions. Reconcile conflicting accounts against observed cases rather than assuming one perspective describes the whole process.']]
},
'process-mapping':{
 sections:[['Model sequence, responsibility and information together','A useful map shows the trigger, activities, responsible roles, decisions, handoffs, outputs and end states. Use a consistent level of detail. A high-level supplier-to-customer map helps agree scope; a detailed flow supports redesign and exception analysis. Swimlanes make role transitions visible but do not prove accountability is correct. In BPMN, sequence flow expresses order within a process, whereas message flow expresses communication between participants. Use notation deliberately: an exclusive choice selects a path, while parallel branching creates work that may need synchronization.'],['Diagnose queues and rework, not just task durations','Elapsed time includes active work and waiting. Five ten-minute tasks can still take days when work sits in queues. Map loops such as return for correction and distinguish avoidable defects from legitimate complex cases. Identify the constraint limiting total throughput before optimizing individual steps. Increasing upstream output can simply enlarge a downstream queue. Separate the observed current state from the proposed future state and validate both with people who perform the work. Define what happens when a decision cannot be made or required information is unavailable.']],
 compare:[['Current-state map','Describes observed work and variation.','Should include workarounds, not only policy.'],['Future-state map','Describes the proposed operating design.','Requires validation and feasible controls.'],['Exclusive gateway','Selects an alternative path by condition.','Conditions need clear coverage and defaults.'],['Parallel gateway','Creates or synchronizes concurrent paths.','An incorrect join can cause waiting or deadlock.']],
 case:{situation:'A new process sends an application to credit review and identity review in parallel. Some low-risk cases skip credit review, but the final approval waits for both completion signals in every case.',question:'Why might the future-state process stall?',reasoning:'The join expects a signal from a branch that some cases never enter. Model the conditional path and its matching join consistently. Test low-risk, high-risk, failed-review and unavailable-information cases before deployment. The visual appeal of a parallel design does not establish its logical correctness.',alternatives:'Increasing staff cannot produce a completion event for a task that was never created. A timer can expose the stall but does not correct the process logic.'},
 checks:[['What does a handoff reveal beyond a role change?','Potential queue time, loss of context, unclear responsibility and repeated data entry. Examine information and acceptance criteria at the handoff.'],['Why can a fast average hide poor service?','Averages hide distribution and outliers. Inspect percentiles, aged cases and case segments rather than relying on one aggregate.']]
},
'workflow-design':{
 sections:[['Specify routing and exception behavior','Routing rules should state the condition, destination, owner, due time and fallback. Validate thresholds at their boundaries and decide what happens when required data is absent or conflicting. Serial approval supports ordered dependencies; parallel review can reduce waiting where work is independent. Escalation needs a responsible recipient and a decision rule, not just repeated notifications. Preserve authorization checks when tasks are delegated. Record enough event history to reconstruct who acted, what changed and why, while avoiding unnecessary sensitive detail in logs.'],['Measure service without encouraging shortcuts','Define throughput, elapsed time, active effort, error rate, rework and control failures separately. A team can increase completion counts by closing incomplete cases, so pair speed with quality and outcome measures. Service levels should identify the clock start, clock stop, working calendar and treatment of external waits. Measure queue aging and exceptions to reveal cases that completion-only metrics miss. If observed demand exceeds capacity, distinguish temporary peaks from persistent constraints before adding resources or redesigning the flow.']],
 compare:[['Assignment','Makes someone responsible for a task.','Does not mean the task is completed.'],['Notification','Informs a recipient of an event.','Is not a reliable substitute for owned work.'],['Escalation','Changes attention or authority after a condition.','Needs limits and an accountable recipient.'],['Audit event','Records an action and context.','Must be protected against unauthorized changes.']],
 case:{situation:'A workflow escalates overdue requests to a manager, but reassignment removes the original deadline and resets the clock. Reports show improved compliance even though customers wait longer.',question:'How should the design and measurement change?',reasoning:'Preserve the original service start and overall deadline alongside task-level dates. Record reassignment and its reason. Report total elapsed time and aged work in progress. Escalation should help resolve delay rather than erase its evidence. Validate that authorized delegates can act without bypassing separation of duties.',alternatives:'More reminders do not repair the measurement defect. Comparing only the latest assignee duration rewards administrative resets instead of service improvement.'},
 checks:[['When is parallel review unsuitable?','When one review requires the result of another, or concurrency creates conflicting changes that cannot be reconciled safely.'],['What should happen when routing data is missing?','Send the case to a visible, owned exception path with correction criteria; do not silently choose a permissive default.']]
},
'rpa-and-cases':{
 sections:[['Match the technology to the work pattern','RPA is useful for repetitive, rule-based interaction with stable interfaces, particularly where suitable integration is unavailable. An API can be more robust when supported because screen coordinates, labels and session behavior change. Workflow coordinates defined tasks and handoffs. Case management supports a persistent case with information, goals and possible actions when the next step depends on professional judgment. These approaches can work together: a caseworker may invoke a bot for routine retrieval inside a broader case.'],['Treat bots as controlled operational actors','Assign ownership, least-privilege credentials, secure secret storage, monitoring and incident response. Separate development, approval and production changes. A bot needs exception handling for unexpected screens, incomplete inputs, duplicate submissions and partial completion. Design retries so the same business transaction is not posted twice. Track whether a transaction was actually committed before repeating it. Calculate support, maintenance and control effort as well as labor savings; brittle automation may shift effort into difficult-to-diagnose failures.']],
 compare:[['RPA','Automates repeatable interface interactions.','Sensitive to interface and business-rule changes.'],['API integration','Exchanges structured requests through supported contracts.','Still needs authorization, error handling and version control.'],['Workflow','Coordinates predictable stages and responsibilities.','Rigid paths can constrain genuinely variable work.'],['Case management','Supports evidence and judgment around a case goal.','Still requires ownership, boundaries and auditability.']],
 case:{situation:'A bot enters refunds into a legacy application. It times out after clicking Submit, so it automatically repeats the entire transaction. Customers sometimes receive two refunds.',question:'What control is missing?',reasoning:'The automation cannot distinguish an uncommitted request from a completed transaction with a lost response. Use a unique transaction reference, check committed status, make operations idempotent where possible and route ambiguous results to an exception queue. Test recovery after each point of partial completion.',alternatives:'A longer timeout may reduce frequency but does not prevent duplicates. Shared administrator credentials increase risk. Ignoring ambiguous transactions conceals financial errors.'},
 checks:[['Is a judgment-heavy investigation a poor candidate for every form of automation?','No. Keep judgment with accountable people while automating stable supporting tasks such as retrieval, reminders or validation.'],['What makes a bot maintenance plan necessary?','Application changes, credentials, rules, volumes and exceptions evolve. Someone must detect failures, approve changes and maintain recovery procedures.']]
},
'signatures':{
 sections:[['Separate intent from cryptographic evidence','An electronic signature is a method used to indicate agreement or approval electronically. A digital signature uses cryptography to support integrity and signer association. Neither term alone establishes legal enforceability for every transaction. Consider the applicable requirements, signer identity, intent, consent to electronic handling, association with the exact content and ability to retain and reproduce evidence. A typed name can have evidential value in an appropriate process; a certificate does not by itself prove that the human understood or intended the transaction.'],['Preserve the evidence package','A signed document needs the signed content, relevant signature data, timestamps, certificate information and process history required to assess it later. Validation at signing and validation years later are different challenges: certificates expire, revocation information changes and algorithms age. A trusted timestamp can help establish when a signature existed; it is not a substitute for an appropriate identity process. Plan long-term validation and export before choosing a service. Access to the signing portal should not be the only way to demonstrate what was agreed.']],
 compare:[['Electronic signature','Broad electronic indication of approval or intent.','Assurance depends on the surrounding process.'],['Digital signature','Cryptographic mechanism supporting integrity and signer association.','Private-key control and certificate trust matter.'],['Authentication','Checks an asserted identity.','Does not by itself establish consent to a specific document.'],['Timestamp','Provides evidence relating an event to time.','Its trust and scope must be evaluated.']],
 case:{situation:'A vendor exports signed contracts as flattened PDFs without signature validation data. It says its portal will retain the audit trail, but the organization plans to terminate the subscription.',question:'What should the organization secure before exit?',reasoning:'Obtain a usable, verifiable evidence package and test independent retrieval and validation. Confirm the export includes the exact signed versions, signature and timestamp information and relevant audit evidence. Apply appropriate retention and access controls. Establish who can explain the evidence if challenged later.',alternatives:'A screenshot of a green checkmark is weak evidence of validation. Retaining a hash alone cannot show signer intent or reconstruct the transaction.'},
 checks:[['Does a valid digital signature guarantee that a document is true?','No. It can support integrity and signer association; a signer can still sign inaccurate content.'],['Why is identity assurance risk-based?','A low-impact internal acknowledgement and a high-value external agreement can justify different authentication, witnessing and evidence requirements.']]
}
});

Object.assign(depth,{
'governance':{
 sections:[['Translate principles into decisions people can execute','Governance establishes who can decide, what standards apply, how conflicts are resolved and what evidence demonstrates compliance. A policy is a direction; a standard makes required conditions specific; a procedure describes how work is performed. Assign an accountable business owner for outcomes, operational stewards for quality and technology custodians for system operation. Responsibilities may overlap in small organizations, but decision rights must remain clear. A committee without authority, resources or a dispute-resolution path can become a meeting schedule rather than a governance mechanism.'],['Use a portfolio of controls and measures','Map obligations, business needs and risks to controls, then identify an owner and evidence for each control. Prioritize critical information and high-impact processes instead of demanding uniform perfection everywhere. Review exceptions with an expiry date, compensating controls and a named approver. Metrics should show outcomes such as reliable retrieval, reduced exposure and timely disposition, not just how many policies were written. Review governance after incidents, acquisitions, new services and legal or organizational changes; an old policy can remain technically published while no longer matching operations.']],
 compare:[['Policy','Sets direction and mandatory expectations.','Needs authority and implementation.'],['Standard','Defines specific required conditions.','Must be measurable enough to apply consistently.'],['Procedure','Describes operational steps.','Must reflect actual systems and responsibilities.'],['Guideline','Offers recommended practices.','Should not be confused with a mandatory control.']],
 case:{situation:'Legal wants longer retention, privacy wants less personal information and operations wants rapid access. IT is asked to choose one rule and configure it globally without a business decision.',question:'How should governance resolve this?',reasoning:'Identify the relevant information categories, purposes, obligations and risks. Bring accountable stakeholders together through established decision rights. Approve differentiated requirements and record their rationale, ownership and review date. IT implements the authorized rule and tests it; it should not silently resolve competing legal and business interpretations.',alternatives:'The longest period is not automatically safest. Allowing every department an undocumented exception makes consistent implementation and assurance impossible.'},
 checks:[['How is accountability different from activity ownership?','A person may perform a task while another role is answerable for the outcome and authorized to approve tradeoffs. Make both explicit.'],['What makes an exception defensible?','A documented reason, scope, approver, risk assessment, compensating controls, expiry and review, rather than an indefinite informal waiver.']]
},
'security-controls':{
 sections:[['Match the protection to the failure mode','Confidentiality prevents unauthorized disclosure, integrity protects against inappropriate alteration and availability supports timely authorized use. Controls often support more than one goal, but none solves every threat. Encryption helps protect content under defined conditions; it does not correct excessive access by an authorized user. Authentication checks identity while authorization limits permitted actions. Apply least privilege and separation of duties to user accounts, administrators, service identities and automation. Review effective permissions, including inherited access and shared links, rather than trusting a nominal group name.'],['Protect recovery as well as production','Backups need controlled access, appropriate separation, monitoring and tested restoration. A successful backup job does not prove the business can recover within its required time or restore consistent information. Distinguish recovery time objective, the acceptable restoration time, from recovery point objective, the acceptable data-loss interval. Logging must capture useful evidence without exposing credentials or unnecessary personal information. Protect logs against unauthorized alteration and synchronize time sources appropriately. Patch management, access review and incident response are continuing responsibilities rather than one-time configuration tasks.']],
 compare:[['Preventive control','Reduces the chance of an unwanted action.','Can fail and needs complementary detection.'],['Detective control','Reveals suspicious or incorrect activity.','Requires someone to respond.'],['Corrective control','Restores or remedies an affected state.','Must be tested and operationally feasible.'],['Compensating control','Addresses a risk when the preferred control is unavailable.','Needs an explicit rationale and review.']],
 case:{situation:'A repository is encrypted, but every employee belongs to a group that can download personnel files. The team proposes stronger encryption as the main remediation.',question:'What should receive priority?',reasoning:'Correct authorization and exposure: identify legitimate roles, restrict groups and links, review actual access and investigate inappropriate downloads. Preserve relevant evidence and follow the incident process. Stronger cryptography does not stop an authorized application from delivering decrypted content to overly broad users.',alternatives:'Removing all logs would make investigation harder. Restricting only the screen while leaving export APIs open creates inconsistent enforcement.'},
 checks:[['Can a daily backup meet a one-hour data-loss requirement?','Not by itself. The recovery design needs sufficiently frequent or continuous protection and a tested restore path consistent with that requirement.'],['Why review service accounts?','They may hold broad, long-lived access outside ordinary employee reviews and can become a significant route to unauthorized actions.']]
},
'privacy-by-design':{
 sections:[['Connect each data element to a justified purpose','Start with the purpose and the people affected, then identify the minimum information required. Distinguish information needed to deliver a service from optional analytics or marketing. Explain uses clearly and assess the applicable basis and individual rights for the context; consent is not a universal substitute for every other requirement. Privacy risks include unfair use, inference and loss of autonomy even when confidentiality is intact. Map collection, sharing, storage, access and disposal across vendors and internal systems so the notice matches actual behavior.'],['Design practical limits and rights workflows','Set retention by purpose and applicable obligations, with triggers, review and exceptions. Pseudonymization replaces direct identifiers but can still permit linkage; it should not casually be called anonymization. Aggregate results can reveal people in small groups, so evaluate re-identification risk and disclosure context. Plan identity verification, scope, searching, response and recording for rights requests without collecting excessive new identity evidence. Consider conflicts such as another person’s privacy or a preservation duty through an authorized review. A request button is insufficient if nobody can locate downstream copies.']],
 compare:[['Minimization','Limits collection and use to justified needs.','Does not mean deleting evidence subject to a valid duty.'],['Pseudonymization','Replaces direct identifiers with a separable reference.','Linkability can keep it personal information.'],['Anonymization','Aims to prevent identification in the relevant context.','Requires assessing realistic re-identification routes.'],['Privacy impact assessment','Examines processing effects and mitigations.','Must inform design and be revisited when processing changes.']],
 case:{situation:'A training app wants to retain names, detailed answers and device locations forever to improve lessons. Its reports are described as anonymous because student names are replaced by account numbers.',question:'How should the design improve?',reasoning:'Define the actual analysis needs, remove unnecessary location collection, limit identifiable learning records and use carefully evaluated aggregation for longer-term trends. Account numbers linked to student records are pseudonymous, not necessarily anonymous. Publish accurate purposes and retention, control access and assess whether small groups reveal individuals.',alternatives:'A vague notice about improving services does not justify collecting everything indefinitely. Encrypting the dataset does not resolve excessive collection or retention.'},
 checks:[['Why is privacy broader than security?','A securely protected dataset can still be used for an incompatible or unfair purpose. Privacy examines whether and how processing should occur.'],['What changes should trigger reassessment?','New purposes, sensitive information, profiling, new recipients, different locations or material changes in scale and technology can change the risk and applicable requirements.']]
},
'sovereignty-and-incidents':{
 sections:[['Map practical access, not just storage geography','A stated hosting region does not reveal every place information may be processed or accessed. Investigate backups, support access, subprocessors, telemetry, remote administration and export routes. Jurisdiction can depend on parties and processing context as well as physical location. Distinguish a residency commitment from broader legal control or transfer requirements. Obtain qualified advice for specific obligations and verify vendor commitments against technical configurations. A contract and a region dropdown are both relevant, but neither alone establishes that every requirement is satisfied.'],['Prepare decisions before the incident','An incident plan names roles, escalation channels, evidence handling, containment authority, communication and recovery criteria. Confirm what happened and preserve useful evidence while limiting ongoing harm. Notification duties depend on the applicable regime, facts and affected information; do not assume one universal deadline. Record the incident timeline and decisions. Restore safely, validate integrity and monitor recurrence. Afterward, identify underlying control and process failures and track improvements to completion. A lesson-learned meeting without owned actions rarely changes the next response.']],
 compare:[['Residency','Where information is stored or processed.','Does not fully describe legal jurisdiction.'],['Sovereignty','Legal authority affecting information and its handling.','Cannot be inferred from server location alone.'],['Containment','Limits ongoing incident effects.','Should consider evidence and business impact.'],['Recovery','Restores trusted operations.','Requires validation, not merely restarting systems.']],
 case:{situation:'A cloud contract promises regional storage, but support staff in another region can open customer files and backups replicate elsewhere. After a suspected breach, an administrator wants to wipe the affected machine immediately.',question:'What two assumptions need correction?',reasoning:'Storage location alone does not describe access or replication, so reassess the complete data flow and commitments. During response, coordinate containment with evidence preservation and authorized recovery decisions. Wiping may destroy investigation evidence; the appropriate action depends on ongoing harm and the incident plan.',alternatives:'Assuming all cross-border activity is automatically prohibited is as unreliable as assuming it is always permitted. Leaving an attack active solely to preserve evidence can also be inappropriate.'},
 checks:[['What vendor evidence is useful beyond a marketing claim?','Relevant contract terms, subprocessor information, access controls, actual configuration, audit evidence and tested export or deletion arrangements.'],['Why assign notification decision owners?','Technical responders may establish facts, while authorized privacy, legal and business roles determine applicable communications and obligations.']]
},
'records':{
 sections:[['Recognize evidence by function and context','A record documents activity, decisions, rights or obligations; file type and location alone do not determine status. An informal message can be important evidence, while a formal-looking duplicate may add little independent value. Identify the business event, content, participants, relationships and authority needed to interpret it. Declaring or capturing a record should preserve relevant context and controls, not merely make a file read-only. Authenticity concerns whether it is what it claims to be; reliability concerns its usefulness as evidence of the activity.'],['Automate classification with explicit assurance','Rules and classifiers can apply record categories at scale, but metadata quality, ambiguous content and changing business activities create errors. Evaluate incorrect inclusion and exclusion separately because their consequences differ. Test representative examples and difficult exceptions before broad deployment. Set an ownership and correction process, preserve decision history and review changes. A high overall accuracy figure may conceal systematic failure for a rare but sensitive record class. Assess the lifecycle effect: a classification error can alter access, retention, discovery and disposal.']],
 compare:[['Authenticity','Supports the claimed identity and origin of evidence.','A document can be authentic but contain an error.'],['Reliability','Supports trustworthy evidence of an activity.','Depends on creation and business controls.'],['Integrity','Protects completeness and appropriate change.','Legitimate changes need traceable controls.'],['Usability','Supports location, retrieval and interpretation.','Intact bits without context may be unusable.']],
 case:{situation:'A project team treats only signed PDFs as records. A chat discussion approves a major scope change, while an unsigned spreadsheet records the agreed financial impact. Neither is captured.',question:'What should the records approach change?',reasoning:'Classify by business function and evidence requirements rather than format. Capture the relevant decision and supporting context, including relationships to the project, participants and approved change. Define practical triggers and responsibilities across collaboration channels and verify capture completion.',alternatives:'Capturing every chat forever creates unnecessary volume and exposure. Converting everything to PDF can lose meaningful structure and relationships.'},
 checks:[['Does making a file read-only establish authenticity?','It is only one possible control. Origin, identity, context, custody and audit evidence are also relevant.'],['Why can 98 percent classification accuracy still be unacceptable?','The remaining errors may concentrate in critical categories, or false exclusions may trigger premature deletion. Evaluate risk by class and error type.']]
},
'retention':{
 sections:[['Build a rule that can actually run','A schedule needs a defined category, accountable authority, trigger event, period, final action and exceptions. Event-based retention cannot be calculated reliably when the event is missing or ambiguous. Identify the source of the authoritative close date, who corrects it and how reopened matters are handled. Distinguish minimum preservation needs from limits on unnecessary retention. Do not invent one universal number of years for a document type without its jurisdiction, activity and applicable requirements. Document the basis and approval for the schedule.'],['Make disposition verifiable and reversible until authorized','Eligibility is not the same as authorization to destroy. Check holds, open matters, exceptions and required reviews before disposition. Record what was disposed, under which approved rule, when and by whose authority without retaining the sensitive content unnecessarily. Test rules on representative collections in a non-destructive mode and review unexpected results. Backups need their own documented lifecycle and restoration controls; deleting an active copy does not mean every backup is immediately erased. Preserve deletion commitments through restore and system retirement.']],
 compare:[['Creation trigger','Starts a clock when an item is created.','May be unsuitable for obligations tied to later events.'],['Event trigger','Starts a clock at a defined business event.','Requires reliable event evidence and handling of missing dates.'],['Hold','Suspends routine disposition for relevant information.','Does not replace the underlying retention schedule.'],['Disposition evidence','Documents an authorized lifecycle action.','Should prove the action without unnecessarily preserving disposed content.']],
 case:{situation:'A contract schedule runs from termination, but a migration maps upload dates into the termination field. The disposal engine marks thousands of contracts eligible, including several still active.',question:'What should happen before any deletion?',reasoning:'Stop the affected disposition, preserve the audit trail and identify the mapping defect. Reconstruct authoritative event dates and validate active or reopened cases. Recalculate eligibility in a non-destructive test, check holds and obtain appropriate authorization. The engine followed its inputs; business evidence must establish the correct trigger.',alternatives:'Adding a year to every date hides rather than fixes the defect. Retaining everything forever avoids this one deletion but creates other costs and risks.'},
 checks:[['What happens when a required trigger date is unknown?','Route it to an owned exception process and prevent unsupported automatic disposition. Do not silently substitute a convenient date.'],['Why coordinate schedules with privacy?','Retention must meet justified preservation needs while limiting unnecessary personal information. Conflicts require authorized, contextual resolution.']]
},
'holds-and-discovery':{
 sections:[['Preserve relevant information across real systems','A hold should identify the matter, custodians, information scope, systems, time ranges and responsible roles. Communicate obligations and suspend conflicting deletion, including automation and relevant departing-user workflows. Track acknowledgement and practical implementation; sending a notice does not prove preservation occurred. Review scope as facts change and release the hold through authorized instructions. Shared drives, cloud collaboration, messages, devices and third-party services may all contain relevant evidence; a repository-only search can miss important sources.'],['Maintain evidence and proportional scope','Collection should preserve useful content, metadata and relationships while documenting methods, dates, handlers and transformations. Chain of custody explains handling; it does not compensate for an incomplete or unreliable collection method. Protect originals and work on controlled copies where appropriate. Search and review must balance relevance, privilege, privacy and proportionality under responsible legal direction. Track exceptions and test completeness. Time zones, date fields, conversation threading and duplicate treatment can materially change results, so record assumptions instead of relying on unexplained defaults.']],
 compare:[['Preservation','Prevents loss or inappropriate alteration.','Broader than sending a hold notice.'],['Collection','Acquires information by controlled methods.','Must preserve relevant context and document exceptions.'],['Review','Assesses relevance, sensitivity or other criteria.','Needs authorized criteria and quality assurance.'],['Production','Delivers agreed information in a usable form.','Requires validation of format, scope and protections.']],
 case:{situation:'A legal hold covers a departing employee. IT preserves the mailbox but deletes the collaboration account under its normal offboarding rule. The employee’s project messages and shared-file permissions are lost.',question:'Where did the control fail?',reasoning:'The hold was not translated into cross-system operational controls. Map the custodian’s information sources, coordinate offboarding with preservation and verify actual suspension of deletion. Investigate recoverability and document any loss. Integrate future hold checks into lifecycle workflows instead of depending on separate informal communications.',alternatives:'A signed acknowledgement does not recover missing data. Preserving every employee account forever is not a well-scoped substitute for coordinated holds.'},
 checks:[['Does releasing a hold authorize immediate deletion of everything it covered?','No. Reapply the applicable schedule and any other holds or constraints, then follow authorized disposition procedures.'],['Why record collection tool and settings?','They help explain completeness, transformations and limitations, and support repeatability when the evidence or method is questioned.']]
},
'manage-in-place':{
 sections:[['Central policy does not require central storage','Manage-in-place applies governance to information in its existing repositories. It can reduce unnecessary movement and preserve familiar work patterns, but depends on each repository’s ability to enforce classification, access, retention, holds and audit requirements. A central inventory or search index is not itself control over the underlying object. Determine whether connectors only discover content, write metadata or can actually enforce lifecycle actions. Test failures, offline sources and changed permissions. Define how conflicts between a central policy and local settings are resolved.'],['Evaluate the full operating lifecycle','Map control coverage by source and information class, including exports and unmanaged copies. Monitor connector health, policy versions, exceptions and enforcement evidence. Access-aware search must not reveal sensitive snippets from sources the requester cannot use. A connector with broad service permissions can become a concentration of risk and should be carefully scoped. Plan source retirement, vendor exit and evidence preservation. If a system cannot enforce a necessary control, consider compensating controls or a justified move rather than assuming federation makes it compliant.']],
 compare:[['Federated search','Finds information across sources.','Does not automatically enforce retention or holds.'],['Central inventory','Records what exists and where.','Can become stale without reconciliation.'],['Policy orchestration','Coordinates rules across repositories.','Capabilities and evidence differ by connector.'],['Consolidation','Moves selected information into a target.','Adds migration effort and can lose context if poorly designed.']],
 case:{situation:'A governance dashboard shows a hold label on a cloud folder. Testing reveals the connector can set metadata but cannot stop the source’s automatic deletion rule.',question:'Is the hold implemented?',reasoning:'No. A visible label is not evidence of preservation. Suspend deletion through a supported source control or use an approved preservation approach, verify the outcome and document gaps. Monitor enforcement health rather than equating a successful metadata write with compliance.',alternatives:'Refreshing the dashboard more often cannot add an unsupported enforcement capability. Moving everything immediately without testing can introduce separate migration and preservation risks.'},
 checks:[['What is a useful connector acceptance test?','Apply a rule, perform the prohibited or required operation in the source, and verify both enforcement and audit evidence, including failure behavior.'],['When can centralization be appropriate?','When justified business or control requirements exceed source capabilities and the benefits outweigh migration, usability and lifecycle risks.']]
}
});

Object.assign(depth,{
'strategy':{
 sections:[['Make the strategy a set of choices','An information strategy should explain the business outcomes, current constraints, priorities and decisions that guide investment. A vision such as trusted information is useful only when translated into scope, ownership and measures. Connect initiatives to business services, risk and user needs. Distinguish executive sponsorship, which supplies authority and resources, from operational ownership, which sustains outcomes. Identify dependencies among information quality, controls, processes and technology; installing a platform before resolving ownership can multiply inconsistent practices.'],['Sequence a roadmap around value and readiness','A roadmap should identify achievable increments, dependencies, resources, decision gates and review points. Quick wins can build confidence, but should contribute to the intended operating model rather than create permanent disconnected tools. Balance urgent risk reduction with foundations such as inventory, taxonomy, identity and retention. Define what will stop or change if evidence contradicts assumptions. Strategy is maintained through governance: new obligations, acquisitions, budget constraints and service needs can justify reprioritization without abandoning the overall goals.']],
 compare:[['Vision','Describes the desired future.','Needs decisions and measurable outcomes.'],['Strategy','Sets priorities and choices to reach outcomes.','Cannot simply be a list of products.'],['Roadmap','Sequences initiatives and dependencies.','Needs regular review as evidence changes.'],['Operating model','Defines ongoing roles, processes and control.','Must be resourced after implementation.']],
 case:{situation:'Three departments independently buy search, collaboration and archiving tools. Each promises better information management, but identity integration, ownership and classification differ. The executive sponsor asks for a single roadmap.',question:'What should the roadmap establish first?',reasoning:'Agree shared business outcomes, information responsibilities and the capabilities that require coordination. Assess existing investments and dependencies before deciding what to keep, integrate or replace. Prioritize increments with measurable service and risk outcomes, accountable owners and sustainable operating resources.',alternatives:'Replacing every tool immediately may waste useful investment. Declaring all departmental purchases acceptable preserves incompatible foundations and does not create a coherent strategy.'},
 checks:[['What makes a useful strategic measure?','An outcome linked to a baseline and owner, such as reduced retrieval failure for a critical service, rather than a count of purchased licenses.'],['Why identify what is out of scope?','It prevents ambiguous expectations and makes resource and priority tradeoffs visible. Scope changes should then follow a deliberate decision process.']]
},
'baseline':{
 sections:[['Assess people, process, information and technology together','A baseline should describe how work operates, what information exists, where it resides, who uses it and what constraints matter. Include skills, culture, ownership, process variation, integration, infrastructure and controls. Inventory depth should fit the decision: a high-level landscape can guide strategy, while migration requires item and relationship detail. Validate interviews with observation, system evidence and representative samples. Document uncertainty instead of presenting incomplete inventories or self-assessed maturity scores as established fact.'],['Use maturity to choose the next feasible improvement','A maturity model organizes capabilities into levels, but a high score is not automatically a business outcome. Look for evidence that practices are defined, repeatable, measured and improved. Compare like-for-like services and assess critical gaps separately from averages. An organization with strong storage technology but weak ownership may need governance and adoption work before advanced automation. Record the baseline method so later measurements are comparable. Avoid changing definitions halfway through a project merely to make improvements look larger.']],
 compare:[['Inventory','Identifies assets, sources and characteristics.','Completeness and freshness require validation.'],['Baseline','Establishes the starting condition for comparison.','Needs a reproducible measurement method.'],['Gap assessment','Compares current and required capabilities.','Requirements and priorities must be explicit.'],['Maturity assessment','Examines how consistently capabilities operate.','A score does not replace outcome evidence.']],
 case:{situation:'Managers report that staff find documents quickly, but a sample of service requests shows repeated searching and requests for colleagues to resend files. The team records only the managers’ estimates in its baseline.',question:'How should the evidence be reconciled?',reasoning:'Observe representative retrieval tasks and distinguish locating a file from finding the authoritative usable information. Segment by user role and task, record failure and rework, and investigate the difference from self-reports. Use a documented method that can be repeated after change.',alternatives:'Choosing the most optimistic number weakens the business case. A single difficult search should not be generalized to the whole organization either.'},
 checks:[['Why assess existing capabilities before procurement?','The organization may already own suitable tools, or lack prerequisite skills and controls needed to realize the proposed benefits.'],['What should accompany a maturity score?','Evidence, scope, assessment criteria, limitations, priority gaps and practical next actions.']]
},
'business-case':{
 sections:[['Distinguish cash savings, capacity and risk reduction','A credible business case identifies the problem, options, full lifecycle cost, expected benefits, assumptions, risks and ownership. Time saved does not automatically reduce expenditure: it may create capacity only if demand or work allocation can use it. Avoid counting the same benefit twice, such as labor reduction and identical productivity gains. Risk reduction may be valuable without a precise cash estimate; show the assumptions rather than inventing probabilities. Include a do-minimum option and the consequences of deferral.'],['Make calculations transparent and test sensitivity','Total cost includes implementation, licenses, integration, migration, training, support, operations, assurance and exit. Suppose annual gross benefit is $80,000, recurring cost is $20,000 and initial cost is $120,000. Simple annual net benefit is $60,000 and simple payback is two years, before discounting and timing effects. A three-year simple ROI using total cost is ($240,000 minus $180,000) divided by $180,000, or about 33 percent. State the convention and period; ROI formulas vary. Test lower adoption, slower rollout and higher support cost rather than presenting one optimistic figure.']],
 compare:[['Payback','Time until cumulative benefits recover investment.','Simple versions ignore discounting and later value.'],['ROI','Return relative to a stated cost base and period.','Different conventions require explicit assumptions.'],['Net present value','Discounted net cash flows over time.','Sensitive to timing, discount rate and estimates.'],['Total cost of ownership','Lifecycle cost of an option.','A low purchase price can conceal operating and exit cost.']],
 case:{situation:'A proposal claims one hour saved for each of 100 staff per week. It values all of that as payroll savings, although staffing will remain unchanged and adoption is estimated at 50 percent.',question:'How should the benefit be represented?',reasoning:'Model realistic use and adoption, validate time savings and identify how released capacity improves outcomes. Separate actual expenditure reduction from capacity and service benefits. Assign a benefits owner and measure realized use and outcomes after rollout. Include training and ongoing support costs.',alternatives:'Multiplying maximum savings by every employee overstates realization. Ignoring non-cash service benefits understates legitimate value.'},
 checks:[['What is a sensitivity analysis for?','It tests how changing uncertain assumptions affects the decision and reveals which assumptions deserve validation first.'],['Who should own benefits after launch?','An accountable business role that can change operations and measure realization, rather than leaving all benefit responsibility with the technology vendor.']]
},
'requirements':{
 sections:[['Write requirements around observable outcomes','A requirement should identify the user or business need, expected behavior, conditions and acceptance evidence. Functional requirements describe capabilities; nonfunctional requirements describe qualities and constraints such as performance, accessibility, security and recoverability. Replace vague wording such as fast or user-friendly with agreed measures and representative tasks. Trace requirements to a source, rationale, priority and test. Distinguish mandatory constraints from preferences so procurement does not treat a convenient feature as equivalent to a control requirement.'],['Evaluate evidence under realistic conditions','A vendor demonstration proves only the demonstrated scenario. Use representative data, volumes, user roles, exceptions and integration conditions to test fit. Assess configuration versus customization, supported interfaces, export completeness, accessibility and lifecycle cost. Weighted scoring can support comparison, but a high total should not cancel a failed mandatory requirement. Record assumptions and contractual commitments. Manage changes through impact analysis across cost, schedule, controls, data and training rather than accepting every request as a harmless addition.']],
 compare:[['Functional requirement','Specifies what the solution must do.','Needs defined conditions and expected results.'],['Nonfunctional requirement','Specifies a quality or operating constraint.','Must be testable in an agreed context.'],['Acceptance criterion','Defines evidence that a requirement is met.','Should be agreed before evaluating results.'],['Traceability','Connects need, requirement, implementation and test.','Helps expose omissions and change impacts.']],
 case:{situation:'A vendor earns the highest weighted score, but cannot export audit history required by a mandatory exit requirement. The team suggests accepting the total score because the user interface is excellent.',question:'What should the selection process do?',reasoning:'Treat the failed mandatory requirement as a material gap requiring a supported remedy, authorized change or rejection. Verify any proposed workaround and its cost, evidence and contract terms. Weighted preferences do not automatically override a mandatory constraint.',alternatives:'A verbal roadmap promise is not equivalent to a tested current capability. Changing the weights after seeing the result undermines the comparison.'},
 checks:[['Improve the requirement: retrieval must be fast.','Specify the representative queries, dataset, user permissions, concurrent load, measured percentile response time and acceptable failure rate with stakeholder agreement.'],['Why retain traceability after selection?','It supports acceptance, defect assessment, change control and evidence that the delivered system meets the original business needs.']]
},
'implementation':{
 sections:[['Prepare a controlled transition','Implementation includes configuration, integration, data preparation, testing, training, communication, support and operational handover. Use pilots with representative users and realistic exceptions, not only enthusiastic experts. Define acceptance, rollback and escalation criteria before cutover. A phased rollout reduces the scale of each transition but may create temporary dual systems and reconciliation needs. A big-bang cutover concentrates risk and requires stronger readiness evidence. Neither method is universally best; select based on dependencies, service continuity and ability to recover.'],['Sustain adoption and verify benefits','Training should follow actual role-based tasks, including exceptions and support routes. Explain why the work changes and provide opportunities to practice. Adoption is more than logins: measure completion of intended tasks, quality, workarounds and service outcomes. Assign ongoing ownership for configuration, policies, integrations, content quality and release changes. Review benefits against the baseline after users have had a realistic opportunity to adopt. Feed incidents and feedback into a prioritized improvement process with owners and completion evidence.']],
 compare:[['Pilot','Tests an implementation on a controlled representative scope.','Can mislead if only easy cases are selected.'],['User acceptance testing','Checks agreed business outcomes and workflows.','Is distinct from technical unit testing.'],['Operational readiness','Confirms people, support and controls can run the service.','A successful installation alone is insufficient.'],['Benefits realization','Checks whether intended value actually occurs.','Continues beyond go-live.']],
 case:{situation:'A pilot succeeds with ten specialists and clean sample documents. During organization-wide rollout, users encounter missing metadata, accessibility barriers and an unavailable support team. Leadership calls it a training problem.',question:'How should the team respond?',reasoning:'Assess pilot representativeness and readiness across data, accessibility, workflows and support. Prioritize blocking defects and provide role-appropriate guidance. Adjust rollout scope or pace using agreed criteria. Measure actual task outcomes and investigate workarounds rather than assuming every failure is user resistance.',alternatives:'More generic training does not repair inaccessible controls or broken data. Declaring technical deployment complete does not establish operational success.'},
 checks:[['What belongs in operational handover?','Accountable owners, support and escalation, monitoring, recovery, access administration, documentation, change control and known issues.'],['Why compare outcomes with the original baseline?','It reveals whether benefits occurred and whether improvements came with new defects, costs or risks. Comparable definitions make the assessment credible.']]
}
});
